NDY3NTI=', 'text': '💥\n\nA critical look at modern genetics, DNA science, and biotechnology reveals several troubling inconsistencies. First, the process of nucleic acid isolation seems suspect—using the same simple methods to extract DNA from plants, animals, bacteria, and even viruses raises questions about whether these procedures truly isolate nucleic acids from their nuclei.\n\nThe iconic double helix structure of DNA, as proposed by Watson and Crick, also lacks convincing physical evidence. Their Nobel-winning work, based on salt crystals, doesn’t prove that DNA itself forms a crystal structure or a double helix, making the claim appear more theoretical than factual. DNA’s supposed role in protein synthesis is equally questionable. Simple chemical reactions can explain protein behavior without the need for DNA “coding,” which seems like an unnecessary layer of complexity.\n\nMoreover, current gene sequencing methods, especially those using machines like Illumina’s, rely heavily on software interpretation. Sequencing broken, tangled strands of DNA en masse without understanding their physical arrangement is highly problematic. Critics argue that these sequencers produce arbitrary data, with the actual sequences being a product of the software rather than the sample itself.\n\nPCR, the method used to amplify DNA, is another point of contention. It’s hard to see how something can be amplified a billion times before confirming it’s even present, casting doubt on its utility in genetic testing.\n\nDespite decades of research, there’s little to show in terms of practical applications. Gene editing and therapy, heralded as revolutionary, have failed to produce consistent, useful outcomes. Even paternity and forensic DNA tests, which claim to provide precise results, often yield unreliable or inconsistent data, challenging the notion that DNA is unique or even identifiable across different samples of the same individual.\n\nThese concerns, coupled with a lack of tangible progress in developing DNA-based products, suggest that the entire field of genetics might rest on shaky foundations, relying more on complex software tricks than on actual biological truths.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Wed Jan 22 02:14:05 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1881840343882293585', 'display_text_range': [14, 286], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'media': [{'display_url': 'pic.x.com/Gu5rnyhZDd', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/Alchemywizzard/status/1881888034360328414/photo/1', 'id_str': '1881888031302434816', 'indices': [287, 310], 'media_key': '3_1881888031302434816', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gh3PMZzXQAAHPC6.jpg', 'type': 'photo', 'url': 'https://t.co/Gu5rnyhZDd', 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'features': {'large': {'faces': []}, 'medium': {'faces': []}, 'small': {'faces': []}, 'orig': {'faces': []}}, 'sizes': {'large': {'h': 2048, 'w': 922, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'medium': {'h': 1200, 'w': 540, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'small': {'h': 680, 'w': 306, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'thumb': {'h': 150, 'w': 150, 'resize': 'crop'}}, 'original_info': {'height': 2048, 'width': 922, 'focus_rects': [{'x': 0, 'y': 510, 'w': 922, 'h': 516}, {'x': 0, 'y': 307, 'w': 922, 'h': 922}, {'x': 0, 'y': 243, 'w': 922, 'h': 1051}, {'x': 0, 'y': 0, 'w': 922, 'h': 1844}, {'x': 0, 'y': 0, 'w': 922, 'h': 2048}]}, 'media_results': {'result': {'media_key': '3_1881888031302434816'}}}, {'display_url': 'pic.x.com/Gu5rnyhZDd', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/Alchemywizzard/status/1881888034360328414/photo/1', 'id_str': '1881888031340179456', 'indices': [287, 310], 'media_key': '3_1881888031340179456', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gh3PMZ8XMAA5t44.jpg', 'type': 'photo', 'url': 'https://t.co/Gu5rnyhZDd', 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'features': {'large': {'faces': [{'